 whole story  there that could be told but that would  take up too much time about why why in  those areas Germany also had some  important industrial development going  on as well but as we're going to talk  about it hadn't progressed as far and  the class structure was somewhat  different in Germany than it was in  post-revolutionary France or in England  that was becoming dominated by by the  bourgeoisie or in America so you know  what happens in the development of  modern industrial society well you know  one of the key things that  differentiates industrial society from a  manufacturing society is that machines  are are taking over wider and wider  areas of production so human beings had  been harnessing the power of machine  since the ancient period you know we  have levers water wheels all sorts of  things like that but the Industrial Age  sees this very accelerated progress you  know creating things like like  steamboats creating things like much  better artillery but took a terrible  toll during this the civil war because  they they weren't quite adjusting for it  yet creating medicines creating dyes  creating you know industrial processes  in which parts of the assembly line are  no longer individual human beings but  individual human beings operating  machinery and sometimes machinery even  operating further machinery as we go on  into the late Industrial Age so  this is this transforms the world we  also see massive flooding of people from  the countryside's into the cities in  part because the cities were where most  of the the jobs were the manufacturing  and we see an incredible increase as  well in in poverty and human misery so  there's some really fascinating  developments taking place because all  this massive infrastructure and wealth  and you know things continually being  set in motion is happening but at the  same time it's becoming clear in part  because of better communications just  how miserable the lives of so many of  the people who are playing a role in  this and actually are and that's part of  what Marx is is responding to one of the  other things that's really important to  keep in mind is that there were some  really central political developments  the French Revolution which I've talked  about in several other videos made a  major impact in part by establishing the  bourgeoisie as the the ultimate ruling  party the people who were able to call  the shots and what we see happening in  the move to the modern age is the  decline of formerly privileged classes  like like kings or aristocracy or the  landed gentry or the clergy they end up  losing status and position not  necessarily in terms of rank but a lot  of their rank becomes ceremonial where  the real power lies is in the hands of  the people who are able to generate and  use and keep on developing further  wealth  they're the ones who assume higher and  higher status within society and they're  what we call the bourgeoisie in England  this  this class was rising and it led to some  tensions between aristocrats and you  know the new money as we call it in  America you know since it wasn't an  aristocracy it was it was a bourgeoisie  really running the show from from the  beginning and in France the French  Revolution swept away the previous class  structure and said that it was it you  know trying to liberate the people but  the people turned out to be primarily  the bourgeoisie the people who owned as  we say the means of production so you  see these classes vying for position and  the bourgeoisie is rising to the top in  Germany where Marx is coming from a  place that he's very interested in  what's happening instead is while the  bourgeoisie is rising the nobility is  still holding power and there's tensions  between them and the proletariat are you  know the workers are increasing but  they're in a different power structure  so these these developments mean an  awful lot intellectually Europe had  become one vast network there were  regional characteristics of course and  you can say that there were intellectual  back waters but communications were  vastly improving you know different  movements in philosophy are trickling  into politics into social life into  economics and and vice versa  philosophers are are taking account of  that the three you know dominant  countries as far as thinking the  intellectual life goes are that you know  triangle of England or the Great Britain  rather because it includes Scotland  where a lot was going on France and  Germany with you know the United States  way off you know across the Atlantic and  often importing ideas from from England  in France and then reworking them into  their own interesting forms in  philosophy the German idealists were you  might say the dominance philosophical  movement in the early 19th century but  like most dominant movements they end up  splitting into all different you know  separate camps and if you want a sort of  thumbnail sketch about what was going on  with with German idealism and and hay  galleon philosophy many of them are  going to turn away from idealism and  towards materialism like Marx himself  who claims to turn Hegel on his head  Hegel had thought that there was some  sort of rational process that is running  through history the process of human  development in which consciousness you  know moves from one step or one stage  one shape of consciousness to another  and it does so not by you know everybody  recognizing oh it's time to move the  rather by conflict by the conflict of  ideas born out sometimes even in violent  strife like the French Revolution and  there's a lot of experimentation that  goes on involved in this because we can  only see so far ahead so the French  revolutionaries for example thought that  they were instituting a realm of  absolute freedom against the unfreedom  of the previous monarchy and it's  corruption and it turns out that they  were instituting something that leads to  absolute error as well but that's an  important stepping stone on the stage  for development of humanity and one  thing that Hegel so this is you know the  the dialectical processes we as we call  it or as Hegel called that involves you  know moving from stage to stage to stage  through conflicts of ideas conflicts of  material forces that embody those ideas  against each other leading to greater  and greater synthesis as Hegel called  them now there were a number  of radical hag aliens marks among them  who took parts of Hegel's thought and  then said he was right about that but  he's totally wrong about this and now we  need to rethink this  so Hegel for example had thought that  religion was something very important in  the development of human beings that we  didn't want to get away from religion  but we wanted to sort of turn religion  into something like philosophy religion  was the penultimate stage in the  dialectical development or it's  absolutely knowing marks and feuerbach  before him say no religion is actually  something that's detrimental to human  beings for Bach would talk about it as  as a sign of our own alienation from  ourselves that we project these ideas  these religious ideas out there and we  give them power and we lose ourselves in  the process Marx will actually go so far  as to call religion the opiate of the  masses and by that he doesn't just mean  that they sit around smoking opium all  day feeling good and you know getting  getting high what he means is it's a  palliative for suffering that doesn't  actually address the causes of suffering  it comes out of human suffering as a  response it leaves the suffering in  place and so Marx is drawing upon you  know a notion philosophy that he's  getting from Hegel but he's radically  transforming it in the process he's  interested in thinking about the whole  of things the entire system and how all  of the parts fit together that's very  typically Hegelian he's also interested  in watching how these these processes  develop and trying to get a step ahead  and figure out which side of history we  need to be on there was also an  incredible emphasis at that time on what  we call political economy nowadays we  often call this policymaking or economic  theory or political theory and the idea  was that by by determinate by getting  the right  idea about how things like property or  the laws of supply and demand or how  markets work we could start to improve  the human condition and so people like  Adam Smith would be you know a central  person in in you know political economy  Smith is somebody who Marx actually  spent a lot of time studying and Marx is  is a student of political economy he's  very interested in it  but he thinks that it doesn't go deep  enough into figuring out what's really  going on so in terms of the themes of  our class it doesn't get to the  underlying realities that that prop up  political economy there were also some  other really interesting initiatives  going on you can see this reflected in  the communist manifesto it's you know  discussion of these even what we  nowadays call social theory or social  thought there was quite a bit of  reformist and utopian social thought  being proposed particularly in england  and particularly in France and Marx  spent a lot of time reading people like  charles fourier or SAS emo these are  people who are proposing new kinds of  ways of understanding society and along  with them ways to transform the human  being so that we would eliminate class  conflict and poverty and injustice and  inequalities marx actually came into  contact with some of the people who were  involved in certain of these movements  like the english Chartists now marx is  kind of an interesting character i want  to fit him into this history itself in  part because he is an international  agent of the revolution you might say he  starts out in germany and he's gonna  move to France to Paris and he's gonna  spend a little bit of time in Brussels  and then he's gonna be in in France and  and then in London so he's inhabiting  the key three centers of European  economics European politics  the European life as it's developing in  the mid 19th century to the late 19th  century Germany France England not to  say that other places didn't matter but  those were the places where the the  greatest initiative for changing things  was was occurring he wanted to it looks  like have an academic career at first  but he wasn't able to do so in part  because there was a sort of policy  against allowing people into academic  posts in in the state that he was in who  he had liberal or radical views and and  Marx did so a lot of the young Hegelians  or the radical hug aliens couldn't get  teaching posts because of that and so  Marx ends up going into journalism and  that's indeed how he made a good bit of  the money that he made during his  lifetime was by working as a journalist  for a lot of papers including one based  in in New York never made a ton of money  he actually lived in poverty a good part  of the time some of his children died  young because of that it's kind of a sad  story but he was committed to to living  among the working class and thinking out  the the responses to to what was going  on happened to the working class in  modern industrial society now one other  thing that I do have to mention that is  happening after these two texts are  written but is a very important part of  the story is the revolutions of 1848  1848 was a year in which these uprisings  are taking place and you know when we  talk about revolutions and we're  thinking about you know thinking back to  earlier ones like the American  Revolution or the French Revolution the  idea was that that people were you know  not just rising up in arms and saying  I've had it I can't take it anymore  that's a revolt a revolution attempts to  put a new state of affairs into place to  change the fabric of society so the  American Revolution tried to break  America's  Colony off from the British colonial  power and send America off on its own  course the French Revolution in many  respects even more radical trying to  tear out you know the the first two  estates clergy and nobility from the  French nation and elevate the Third  Estate the people into the positions of  power the revolutions that are taking  place in Germany primarily in 1848 but  but in a few other places as well are  going to aim at expanding human freedom  and there's a lot of different  initiatives going on Marx takes account  of that it's gonna have massive effects  you know people in Russia are going to  crack down after the revolutions  happening over in Europe sending people  like say Dostoevsky to to Siberia as a  result whole complicated story there but  the point is the sort of tensions the  sort of underlying conflicts that are  being discussed came to a critical  juncture point a crisis in 1848 and we  see further things going on and on after  that throughout the rest of the century  and Marx is is is at the heart of quite  a few of those the Communists play a  major role that he's they're not the  only people on the Left they're not the  only people working for revolutions  there are also anarchists and you know  various syndicalists but Marx's ideas  along with angles played a major role in  shaping where history was going to go  let's talk now about some of the key  themes that are going to keep on coming  up that you want to keep an eye out for  and try to at least you know have a good  solid understanding of by the time that  we're finished with this this section so  these are arranged and kind of a  constellation I'm not going to say that  one of them is necessarily more  important than the other because they're  all kind of connected with each other  the first of them  that I would bring up is this notion of  social classes so for Marx human beings  and their consciousness their way of  understanding themselves their way of  being in the world is determined in  large part by how they live and that's  determined by what social class they  belong to among the social classes we  should distinguish two of them as  particularly important to make sense of  one of them is what we call the  bourgeoisie these come from the original  townspeople the burghers in medieval  towns and eventually become the dominant  power the dominant social class within  modern industrial society taking the  other elite classes and either absorbing  them or pushing them away you know just  destroying the the means of their their  power and influence and eventually  dominating the world the other class  that's particularly important to think  about is what's called the proletariat  and the proletariat that's not a word  that's a you know original to Marx it  was used by the Romans to talk about  people who are so dirt poor that the  only thing that they had to contribute  to society was making more babies perlis  and Marx thinks that in modern  industrial society the vast majority of  people are forced into a position of  that sort of poverty where they don't  have opportunities they there's really  you know the whole system is rigged  against them and they they're unable to  accumulate wealth or to put it to work  and their cuz they're you know compelled  to have to work in order to live so  those are two important classes another  key idea is the conflict between social  classes as something that drives the  historical situation that we're in that  explains why things are the  that they are social classes are  involved in relations in which one  social class exploits another social  class and that leads to tensions and  conflict another key idea is that of  alienation or estrangement in German and  from them and this means they a state of  losing something of yourself of having  something of yourself taken away or  seeing it out there as something  standing against you and feeling that  not just thinking about that 